text:
@ORGANIZATION1, on @DATE1 it's clear that technology has become ubiquitous and has positively impacted various aspects of our lives. Many engineers and tech enthusiasts are constantly striving to improve and create devices that make our lives easier and more enjoyable. According to a study conducted by @ORGANIZATION2 university, computers are the most commonly used machines in today's world, contributing to our education, economy, and entertainment.

Speaking of education, computers have become essential tools in schools like the one I attend, where we have over @NUM1 computers for students to use each day. These computers help us complete research papers, assignments, and other required tasks more efficiently. By using computers for research purposes, we save ourselves hours of scanning through books in the library and money on buying books. Moreover, teachers can organize files and enter grades online, making it easier for students to keep track of their progress. The computer's "parent portal" also keeps students informed of school events they may not have heard about otherwise. All in all, computers have made education more accessible and manageable.

But computers don't just benefit education; they also make for affordable entertainment options. With prices starting as low as sixty dollars, there's a computer for everyone. In today's economy, dealing with a depression similar to the one in the past, families are forced to cut back on many expenses. But because computers are more affordable, purchases have been stimulated. Computers have revolutionized entertainment, where instead of using a CD player, we can listen to music while chatting online with friends. This technology is especially helpful for last-minute changes in plans, where communication without a telephone can cause time management issues. Children can even use the internet to play games and de-stress after a hard day at school. Computers also produce the movies and TV shows we watch at home or the videogames we order online. From social media platforms like Facebook and Myspace to classic films like "Gone with the Wind," computers have created a whole new world of entertainment opportunities.

@ORGANIZATION1 and @CAPS1, there is an ongoing debate about computers and their impact on society. Some argue that they are harmful, while others believe they are beneficial. Personally, I am a firm believer in the positive effects of computers, as I think they do more good than harm. One of the biggest benefits of computers is the improvement in hand-eye coordination over time, which can lead to enhanced skills in playing musical instruments. Additionally, they can greatly aid in learning, particularly for students. Without computers, researching for a report would require poring over heavy encyclopedias, whereas with the internet, information is much more easily accessible. Finally, computers are an excellent tool for communication with distant friends and family.

Firstly, I would like to address the issue of hand-eye coordination. This skill involves the ability to use one's hands and eyes simultaneously and can have a significant impact in several areas, including music. When learning a new song on guitar, for instance, hand-eye coordination is crucial in correctly placing one's fingers on the frets. Similarly, in sports like football, coordination is necessary for catching a thrown ball. Moving on to the educational benefits of computers, finding answers to questions is much simpler with their help. For example, if someone asked who the @NUM1 president of @ORGANIZATION2 was, a quick search on a search engine like Google would provide the answer. Additionally, schools can expect more from their students because they have greater access to information.

Lastly, computers are an ideal medium for communication, especially over long distances. If a friend moves out of state, staying in touch can be affordable and convenient through email and other internet-based communication tools. While utilizing phones can be costly, internet communication comes without additional fees, making it a cheaper and more practical option. In conclusion, computers are a crucial part of modern life, and people should be aware of their benefits before arguing against them. Hopefully, this essay will help rectify any misconceptions and encourage a more positive view of computers in society.

Our world @DATE1 is constantly inundated and influenced by different forms of media. From our early childhood, we are exposed to different opinions and beliefs through books, movies, and music that shape our lives. The art industry is known to be one of the most controversial fields to work in, with various critics expressing their views on different pieces of literature or film. While everyone has a right to their opinion, it is impossible to satisfy everyone's needs. People often find certain media content offensive based on personal beliefs or values. For example, religious pieces of art are usually criticized for their non-factual content. Similarly, movies are criticized for representing life situations that do not appeal to everyone. 

Personal tastes can also vary widely when it comes to different genres of media. For instance, some people may enjoy reading about war or watching movies about it, while others, such as war protestors and peacekeepers, may consider it an insult to their cause.  @PERSON1, a noted author, wrote a book @CAPS1 @NUM1, which faced heavy criticism over the issue of censorship. He opined that in a world where people's opinions increasingly dominated everything, any media content can be offensive to some group, leading to books and movies being burnt. Such acts of censorship have been seen throughout history, where books on topics like slavery and war were banned as they were considered offensive by some.

@PERSON1 argues that his book was not about censorship but was meant to convey a more significant message to our generation. We live in a world that is diverse, and each person has an individual and unique mind. Therefore, we must learn to coexist and accept each other's opinions. Our media content shapes our history and becomes a reference for future generations. If we fail to accept and respect diversity, then we will have nothing to improve on, and our lives will stagnate. We must work towards accepting and building on each other's ideas to improve our own and others' lives. We must reflect on our past and learn from our experiences, rather than offend or be offended by others' viewpoints. As we move forward into the future, we must strive to accept the diversity around us and work towards building a better world for everyone.

@PERSON1 was a hamster who had a habit of always wanting what others had. One day, while playing with his owner's sister hamster, @PERSON1 noticed that @CAPS5 had just received a brand new hamster toy filled with tubes to climb in, a slide, and a running wheel. Although @PERSON1 enjoyed playing with his own toys, he couldn't help but wish he had one just like @CAPS5's. Unfortunately, @CAPS1, his owner, didn't have enough money to buy him one yet.

As days passed by, @PERSON1 became increasingly impatient, especially when he saw @PERSON1 happily playing in his new toy. One day, @PERSON1 confided in @PERSON1 and said, "I really wish I had one of those hamster toys, but @CAPS1 doesn't have enough money!" To which @PERSON1 replied, "Don't worry, you can play in mine until he does. I heard he's almost got enough."

Although @PERSON1 was initially hesitant, he decided to take up @PERSON1's offer and play in his toy. A few days later, @PERSON1 was thrilled to receive a brand new toy, which was even bigger than @CAPS4. "I guess @CAPS5 was worth waiting!" he exclaimed. The two hamsters were now able to share the toy and live happily ever after, breaking @PERSON1's habit of always wanting what others had.

Habits in relationships are essential for building trust, communication, and laughter. I firmly believe that laughter is a vital component of any relationship. My best friend and boyfriend, @PERSON2, always manages to put a smile on my face - even on my worst days. I remain grateful to @CAPS1 for bringing him into my life.

@PERSON2's sense of humor was the first thing that caught my attention when we began to get to know each other better. From tickling me until I can barely breathe to making weird faces, he knows how to make me laugh. Even when I'm crying, his silly antics bring a smile to my face. For instance, one terrible day at school, just a few minutes with him cheered me up and made me laugh.

Our ability to laugh together has been the bedrock of our relationship since its inception. We play @CAPS2, and when I fail, we laugh at how terrible I am at it. We even have an inside joke – "That just works out, don't it!" Sometimes, we playfully argue over who gets "the point." However, the goal is to have fun with each other and not to keep score.

@PERSON2 makes funny faces when there's a camera around, leading us to click silly pictures and laugh uncontrollably. The laughter is contagious! Being comfortable together requires the ability to laugh at oneself. So when I kneed myself in the forehead while testing how high we could kick, @PERSON2 was the only one who heard the crack of my bones and burst out laughing. Although I was distraught, his laughter made it easier for me to laugh it off too.

Furthermore, even when we disagree on things, we don't hold grudges. We work through it and later, we can laugh about it. When we made mistakes in the past, we also learned to look back on it, laugh, and move on.

Therefore, I hold that laughter is the shortest distance between two people. @PERSON2's ability to make me laugh is one of the many reasons why I fell in love with him. So between texting "@CAPS3" and watching movies, @PERSON2 and I continue to make each other laugh. As we both often say, "we're freaking soul mates!"
